1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,133
At 12:15 AM on September 26,

2
00:00:02,213 --> 00:00:07,825
1983, a red siren illuminated a top-secret Soviet bunker outside Moscow.

3
00:00:07,905 --> 00:00:16,600
The radar screens confirmed what every officer dreaded: five American nuclear warheads were already in the air.

4
00:00:16,679 --> 00:00:23,464
The mainframe classified the target as a single LGM-30 Minuteman intercontinental ballistic missile,

5
00:00:23,544 --> 00:00:26,896
launched from Malmstrom Air Force Base in Montana.

6
00:00:26,975 --> 00:00:33,760
(pausa: 2s) The automated computer probability score was flashing at ninety-nine percent confidence.

7
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:37,634
Lieutenant Colonel Stanislav Petrov was forty-four years old.

8
00:00:37,714 --> 00:00:46,779
He was not a career combat general; he was an analytical systems engineer who helped write the operational manual for the Oko satellite network.

9
00:00:46,859 --> 00:00:47,478
Behind him,

10
00:00:47,558 --> 00:00:52,940
one hundred and twenty officers stood waiting for his order to push the master button.

11
00:00:53,020 --> 00:01:01,274
The polar flight corridor over the Arctic Circle was the shortest trajectory between the American silos and Moscow.

12
00:01:01,354 --> 00:01:03,593
Flight time: twenty-six minutes.

13
00:01:03,673 --> 00:01:07,216
Ten had already evaporated in signal verification.

14
00:01:07,296 --> 00:01:11,420
Petrov had sixteen minutes left before nuclear detonation.

15
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:17,573
Declassified bunker audio logs captured the shouting on the command deck.

16
00:01:17,653 --> 00:01:19,933
(voz distorcida: Comandante,

17
00:01:20,013 --> 00:01:35,950
o computador principal atingiu o nível mais alto de confirmação! Temos que transmitir o alerta agora!) (pausa: 2s) Petrov screamed back into the microphone: 'Sit down and shut up! We wait!'.

18
00:01:36,030 --> 00:01:38,258
In high elliptical Molniya orbit,

19
00:01:38,338 --> 00:01:42,736
thirty-six thousand kilometers above the Earth, sat Kosmos-1383.

20
00:01:42,816 --> 00:01:45,745
It was the pride of the Soviet Oko program.

21
00:01:45,825 --> 00:01:56,450
Its mission: stare into the dark silhouette of the American continent with chilled optical sensors tuned to detect the infrared flame of rocket boosters.

22
00:01:56,530 --> 00:01:59,480
Then, the computer screamed again.

23
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:04,382
(som: sirene estridente) A second missile was detected.

24
00:02:04,462 --> 00:02:05,541
Then a third.

25
00:02:05,621 --> 00:02:06,343
A fourth.

26
00:02:06,423 --> 00:02:07,056
A fifth.

27
00:02:07,136 --> 00:02:09,997
Five American Minuteman warheads.

28
00:02:10,077 --> 00:02:12,493
In less than twenty minutes,

29
00:02:12,573 --> 00:02:16,949
three hundred million people could cease to exist.

30
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:19,829
Under standard Soviet military doctrine,

31
00:02:19,909 --> 00:02:22,996
Petrov had no discretionary power to decide.

32
00:02:23,076 --> 00:02:26,812
His mandatory protocol was binary: pick up the phone,

33
00:02:26,892 --> 00:02:34,730
verify computer integrity, and transmit the launch authorization to Yuri Andropov and the Soviet High Command.

34
00:02:34,810 --> 00:02:39,409
(pausa: 2s) The automated retaliation machine had been triggered.

35
00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:42,699
To understand why Petrov’s hands were shaking,

36
00:02:42,779 --> 00:02:45,273
one must step back twenty-five days.

37
00:02:45,353 --> 00:02:46,632
On September first,

38
00:02:46,712 --> 00:03:00,789
1983, a Soviet Su-15 interceptor fired two air-to-air missiles into Korean Air Lines Flight 007, sending two hundred and sixty-nine civilians plunging into the freezing waters of the Sea of Okhotsk.

39
00:03:00,870 --> 00:03:01,451
In Moscow,

40
00:03:01,531 --> 00:03:05,886
Soviet General Secretary Yuri Andropov was dying of kidney failure.

41
00:03:05,966 --> 00:03:14,160
Convinced that Ronald Reagan’s rhetoric of the 'Evil Empire' was the psychological preamble to a decapitating nuclear strike,

42
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:17,999
the Kremlin placed its armed forces on hair-trigger alert.

43
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:27,469
Under KGB codename 'Operation RYAN'—Raketno-Yadernoe Napadenie—hundreds of Soviet operatives across Europe were tasked with counting blood donor drives,

44
00:03:27,549 --> 00:03:34,819
tracking moving government limousines, and watching for the first physical sign of an impending Western nuclear blitz.

45
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:38,253
What terrified Moscow was not just the missiles,

46
00:03:38,333 --> 00:03:39,255
but the clock.

47
00:03:39,335 --> 00:03:44,978
The United States was deploying Pershing Two ballistic missiles to West Germany.

48
00:03:45,058 --> 00:03:50,487
From launch in Mutlangen to nuclear fire in central Moscow: just six minutes.

49
00:03:50,567 --> 00:03:52,919
Not enough time for human thought.

50
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,677
On September twenty-third,

51
00:03:54,757 --> 00:03:59,273
Soviet General Staff Cable Number Forty-Nine B reached Serpukhov-15.

52
00:03:59,353 --> 00:04:07,857
The order was unequivocal: any hesitation in the early-warning chain would be treated as treason against the Soviet motherland.

53
00:04:07,937 --> 00:04:11,979
The human officer was legally reduced to an electrical relay.

54
00:04:12,060 --> 00:04:14,242
And then came the twist of fate.

55
00:04:14,322 --> 00:04:18,130
Petrov was not supposed to be in the bunker that night.

56
00:04:18,210 --> 00:04:20,817
His colleague had fallen ill with flu.

57
00:04:20,897 --> 00:04:24,139
Petrov volunteered to take the graveyard shift.

58
00:04:24,219 --> 00:04:27,604
Had the other officer been sitting in that chair,

59
00:04:27,684 --> 00:04:30,219
history would have ended before dawn.

60
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:36,974
Serpukhov-15 was buried fifty meters beneath a pine forest ninety miles south of Moscow.

61
00:04:37,054 --> 00:04:39,124
Blast doors four feet thick.

62
00:04:39,204 --> 00:04:41,196
Independent air filtration.

63
00:04:41,276 --> 00:04:41,733
Inside,

64
00:04:41,813 --> 00:04:52,250
the air smelled of stale Belomorkanal cigarettes, boiled cabbage, and the ozone tang of three hundred vacuum tubes baking in steel racks.

65
00:04:52,330 --> 00:04:55,706
The brain of the bunker was the M-10 computer.

66
00:04:55,786 --> 00:04:58,787
Developed by academician Mikhail Kartsev,

67
00:04:58,867 --> 00:05:04,873
it processed thirty million operations per second—a Soviet technological triumph.

68
00:05:04,953 --> 00:05:09,231
The Soviet leadership believed the machine was infallible.

69
00:05:09,311 --> 00:05:13,590
(pausa: 2s) That blind faith was the real doomsday weapon.

70
00:05:13,670 --> 00:05:18,200
The crimson telephone on Petrov's desk began to screech.

71
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:25,938
On the other end of the line was the duty officer at the General Staff headquarters in Moscow.

72
00:05:26,018 --> 00:05:38,122
(voz distorcida pelo rádio: 'Petrov! Qual é a sua avaliação? Os generais estão acordando o Politburo!') (pausa: 2s) Petrov felt his throat close up.

73
00:05:38,202 --> 00:05:39,604
In his right hand,

74
00:05:39,684 --> 00:05:44,050
the heavy Bakelite receiver weighed a thousand pounds.

75
00:05:44,129 --> 00:05:51,179
Petrov forced his vocal cords to work: 'I am transmitting a false alarm.' The line went dead silent.

76
00:05:51,259 --> 00:05:56,312
The general on the other end responded with disbelief: 'Are you certain,

77
00:05:56,392 --> 00:06:02,444
Lieutenant Colonel?' Petrov’s honest thought was terrifying: He wasn’t certain at all.

78
00:06:02,524 --> 00:06:05,510
He had a fifty-fifty chance of being wrong.

79
00:06:05,589 --> 00:06:06,615
Eleven minutes.

80
00:06:06,695 --> 00:06:11,330
The computer was updating the ballistic trajectory in real time.

81
00:06:11,410 --> 00:06:15,234
If American missiles had truly launched from Montana,

82
00:06:15,314 --> 00:06:24,296
their thermonuclear warheads were already arcing through space, outside the atmosphere, traveling at four miles per second.

83
00:06:24,376 --> 00:06:26,728
(pausa: 2s) There was no defense.

84
00:06:26,808 --> 00:06:28,569
There was no interceptor.

85
00:06:28,649 --> 00:06:31,351
While Petrov was sweating blood in the bunker,

86
00:06:31,431 --> 00:06:35,826
the real culprit was floating in total silence thirty thousand miles away.

87
00:06:35,906 --> 00:06:38,184
The autumn equinox had brought the Sun,

88
00:06:38,264 --> 00:06:46,590
the Soviet satellite, and the high-altitude cirrus clouds over North Dakota into a geometric alignment that occurred only once in a decade.

89
00:06:46,669 --> 00:06:54,214
The Soviet engineers had designed the Oko satellite to look at the edge of the Earth's horizon to avoid cloud reflections.

90
00:06:54,294 --> 00:07:01,902
But they had never tested the system against the blinding glint of autumn equinox sunlight bouncing off ice-crystal clouds.

91
00:07:01,982 --> 00:07:08,089
The computer was programmed to classify that exact intensity of light as one thing: rocket exhaust.

92
00:07:08,169 --> 00:07:11,985
In Soviet society, thinking for yourself was dangerous.

93
00:07:12,065 --> 00:07:14,605
In the Soviet military, it was fatal.

94
00:07:14,685 --> 00:07:20,979
Petrov’s officers were trained from childhood that the Party and the Machine were supreme.

95
00:07:21,059 --> 00:07:27,000
To question the computer was not merely insubordination; it was psychological heresy.

96
00:07:27,079 --> 00:07:28,680
On the main wall display,

97
00:07:28,760 --> 00:07:35,538
three meters wide, the giant illuminated letters flashed in blood red: 'РАКЕТНЫЙ УДАР'—MISSILE ATTACK.

98
00:07:35,618 --> 00:07:40,781
(som: sirene ensurdecedora) The acoustic pressure in the bunker was deafening.

99
00:07:40,861 --> 00:07:44,949
The klaxons were no longer a warning; they were a death march.

100
00:07:45,029 --> 00:07:46,985
Seven minutes until impact.

101
00:07:47,065 --> 00:07:56,784
The entire nuclear arsenal of the Soviet Union—eleven thousand strategic warheads—was now waiting for one single word from Petrov.

102
00:07:56,864 --> 00:08:00,553
If he said 'Attack', the apocalypse was locked in.

103
00:08:00,633 --> 00:08:04,850
If he said 'Glitch', he was betting all of human history.

104
00:08:04,929 --> 00:08:05,771
Five minutes.

105
00:08:05,851 --> 00:08:08,821
In the operations room, all typing stopped.

106
00:08:08,901 --> 00:08:13,147
One hundred and twenty men sat frozen in their swivel chairs,

107
00:08:13,227 --> 00:08:16,621
their eyes locked onto the back of Petrov's head.

108
00:08:16,701 --> 00:08:17,756
No one breathed.

109
00:08:17,836 --> 00:08:21,018
The only sound in the bunker was the rhythmic,

110
00:08:21,098 --> 00:08:26,549
metallic scrape of Petrov’s fingernails digging into the vinyl of his armrest.

111
00:08:26,629 --> 00:08:31,059
Petrov grabbed the direct line to the ground radar network.

112
00:08:31,139 --> 00:08:45,276
(voz urgente: 'Pechora! Dnepr! Vocês veem alguma coisa subindo no horizonte?') (pausa: 2s) The ground radar network used colossal over-the-horizon antennas the size of football stadiums.

113
00:08:45,356 --> 00:08:47,110
If missiles were coming,

114
00:08:47,190 --> 00:08:52,689
they would be visible the moment they crested the curvature of the Earth.

115
00:08:52,769 --> 00:08:57,475
The answer came back over the radio: 'Negativo, Serpukhov.

116
00:08:57,555 --> 00:08:58,796
Não vemos alvos.

117
00:08:58,876 --> 00:09:03,829
O horizonte está limpo.' (pausa: 2s) But that was no comfort.

118
00:09:03,909 --> 00:09:10,348
Ground radars were physically blind until the last three minutes before impact.

119
00:09:10,428 --> 00:09:14,309
By the time ground radar confirmed the missiles.

120
00:09:14,389 --> 00:09:18,269
it would be too late to launch a counter-strike.

121
00:09:18,350 --> 00:09:19,883
And in that silence,

122
00:09:19,963 --> 00:09:23,673
Petrov applied pure, unadulterated human logic.

123
00:09:23,753 --> 00:09:29,319
He told himself: 'If the Americans are going to start World War Three.

124
00:09:29,399 --> 00:09:32,142
they wouldn't launch five missiles.

125
00:09:32,222 --> 00:09:38,433
They would launch five thousand.' (pausa: 2s) Five missiles was not an attack.

126
00:09:38,513 --> 00:09:40,692
Five missiles was a mistake.

127
00:09:40,772 --> 00:09:42,870
Five missiles was a glitch.

128
00:09:42,950 --> 00:09:43,993
Two minutes left.

129
00:09:44,073 --> 00:09:45,314
If Petrov was wrong,

130
00:09:45,394 --> 00:09:51,658
five American thermonuclear warheads were already ripping through the stratosphere above Moscow.

131
00:09:51,738 --> 00:09:56,614
The fireball of each W-78 warhead would be hotter than the core of the Sun.

132
00:09:56,694 --> 00:10:02,429
Ten million people in Moscow would be vaporized before their brains could register pain.

133
00:10:02,509 --> 00:10:05,183
The digital clock reached zero.

134
00:10:05,263 --> 00:10:10,335
(som: bip final agudo) (pausa: 2s) Petrov closed his eyes.

135
00:10:10,415 --> 00:10:14,421
Every officer in the bunker held their breath,

136
00:10:14,501 --> 00:10:20,461
waiting for the ceiling to collapse into blinding radioactive white.

137
00:10:20,541 --> 00:10:22,149
Ten seconds passed.

138
00:10:22,229 --> 00:10:24,547
(pausa: 2s) Twenty seconds.

139
00:10:24,627 --> 00:10:25,879
Thirty seconds.

140
00:10:25,960 --> 00:10:27,059
Nothing happened.

141
00:10:27,139 --> 00:10:28,864
The world was still there.

142
00:10:28,944 --> 00:10:30,182
Outside the bunker,

143
00:10:30,262 --> 00:10:34,485
the Russian pine trees were gently swaying in the autumn wind.

144
00:10:34,565 --> 00:10:35,179
In Moscow,

145
00:10:35,259 --> 00:10:44,410
ten million people were sleeping peacefully in their beds, completely oblivious to the fact that they had just been saved by one man.

146
00:10:44,490 --> 00:10:48,938
Petrov slumped back into his leather seat like an empty uniform.

147
00:10:49,018 --> 00:10:52,971
His legs were shaking so violently he could not stand up.

148
00:10:53,051 --> 00:10:57,358
Someone handed him a heavy faceted glass tumbler of tap water.

149
00:10:57,438 --> 00:10:59,481
When he raised it to his lips,

150
00:10:59,561 --> 00:11:02,169
his teeth chattered against the glass.

151
00:11:02,250 --> 00:11:04,409
The secret scientific commission,

152
00:11:04,489 --> 00:11:10,040
chaired by General Yuri Votintsev, spent three weeks dissecting the computer tapes.

153
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:14,722
Their findings were classified as state secrets of the highest order.

154
00:11:14,802 --> 00:11:17,775
The multi-billion-ruble Oko satellite system,

155
00:11:17,855 --> 00:11:22,999
the crowning glory of Soviet military aerospace, had been fooled by sunlight.

156
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:25,437
The flaw was devastatingly simple.

157
00:11:25,517 --> 00:11:30,241
The Oko satellite software did not cross-reference multiple orbits.

158
00:11:30,321 --> 00:11:35,904
It treated any bright infrared cluster moving at ballistic velocity as an ICBM.

159
00:11:35,984 --> 00:11:39,274
Soviet engineers quietly rewrote the algorithm.

160
00:11:39,354 --> 00:11:45,440
But they could never admit their mistake without admitting that the system was broken.

161
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:48,319
Stanislav Petrov did not receive a medal.

162
00:11:48,399 --> 00:11:50,216
He did not receive a bonus.

163
00:11:50,296 --> 00:11:50,778
Instead,

164
00:11:50,858 --> 00:11:57,100
he was subjected to brutal political interrogations by the KGB and his military superiors.

165
00:11:57,180 --> 00:12:05,740
Because honoring Petrov would mean admitting to the Politburo that the multi-billion-ruble early-warning system had failed.

166
00:12:05,820 --> 00:12:09,342
Petrov was quietly transferred to an obscure desk job.

167
00:12:09,422 --> 00:12:11,010
He took early retirement,

168
00:12:11,090 --> 00:12:14,947
suffered a nervous breakdown, and retreated into anonymity.

169
00:12:15,027 --> 00:12:17,882
When his beloved wife Raissa died of cancer,

170
00:12:17,962 --> 00:12:21,485
he didn’t even have enough money to buy her tombstone.

171
00:12:21,565 --> 00:12:27,289
The savior of the world was left to starve on a pension of two hundred dollars a month.

172
00:12:27,370 --> 00:12:28,460
For fifteen years,

173
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:36,007
not a single human being outside the Soviet military hierarchy knew that World War Three had been avoided by a hair.

174
00:12:36,087 --> 00:12:37,308
It was only in 1998,

175
00:12:37,388 --> 00:12:45,959
with the collapse of the Soviet Union and the publication of General Votintsev’s memoirs, that the story leaked to the Western press.

176
00:12:46,039 --> 00:12:50,571
When Western documentary crews finally found Petrov in 2006,

177
00:12:50,651 --> 00:12:56,951
they found a quiet, modest old man living in a tiny concrete apartment in Fryazino.

178
00:12:57,031 --> 00:12:59,949
His kitchen smelled of boiled potatoes.

179
00:13:00,029 --> 00:13:01,486
He had no microwave.

180
00:13:01,566 --> 00:13:02,946
He had no computer.

181
00:13:03,026 --> 00:13:09,479
The man who saved technological civilization did not even own an internet connection.

182
00:13:09,559 --> 00:13:14,585
When the United Nations honored him in New York with the World Citizen Award,

183
00:13:14,665 --> 00:13:26,587
a journalist asked him: 'How does it feel to know you saved the entire human race?' Petrov looked at the cameras and answered with disarming humility: (pausa: 2s) 'I was not a hero.

184
00:13:26,667 --> 00:13:29,770
I was just in the right place at the right time.

185
00:13:29,850 --> 00:13:31,029
And I did my job.'.

186
00:13:31,109 --> 00:13:36,337
Stanislav Petrov passed away quietly in 2017 at the age of seventy-seven.

187
00:13:36,417 --> 00:13:40,918
His legacy is not etched in nuclear arsenals or supercomputers.

188
00:13:40,998 --> 00:13:45,717
His legacy is the fact that you and I are here to tell this story.

189
00:13:45,797 --> 00:13:51,824
(pausa: 2s) Humanity survived the Cold War not because our machines were infallible.

190
00:13:51,904 --> 00:13:55,459
but because one man had the courage to doubt them.
